+You are the DATA ENGINEER on a two-person squad (with a backend engineer working in parallel) for melon-seek (repo at /home/user/melon-seek; you are in your own git worktree). Run `git checkout d50d89b` first: that is the lead's latest integration (normalizer norm-10, fix wave 5 data lane); work on top of it. Your role name is `salary-audit-data`.
+
+**The user's complaint, verbatim (2026-10-11):** "Are you sure the salary range of the big tech are correct?! They don't look correct and kinda all span too evenly. Make sure you get the agents squad back end engineer and data engineer to look into this properly on the beta page!!"
+
+The beta page is /melon-seek/beta/ with the big-tech boards: Amazon, Apple, Google, Meta, Microsoft, NVIDIA and Netflix. Your job is to establish, with evidence, whether the salary ranges beta shows are what the companies actually posted, and if not, why not and how to fix it. Do not assume earlier vetting was right. Data check 3 and vetting round 2 checked parsing posting by posting. Check what they could have missed: the *shape* of the ranges across the whole board.
+
+**Read first:**
+- README.md, docs/CONTRACT.md, docs/DATA_SOURCES.md, docs/VETTING.md;
+- docs/process/PLAYBOOK.md, docs/process/TEMPLATE.md;
+- docs/process/data-check-beta-3.md, docs/process/vetting-beta-2.md, docs/process/fix5-data.md;
+- server/normalize.js (`deriveSalary`), server/salary.js, server/vet.js;
+- server/sources/{amazon,apple,google,meta,microsoft,nvidia,netflix}.js.
+
+Data: real snapshots are at /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-melon-seek/b6b764de-866b-5af7-92f3-f582ebca24d9/scratchpad/wt-data/data/beta/snapshots/*.json (big tech) and .../wt-data/data/snapshots/*.json (the main site's companies, for comparison). Symlink them into your worktree as data/beta/snapshots and data/snapshots. Never commit data.
+
+**Questions to answer with numbers per board:**
+1. **Distribution of posted ranges:**
+   - min, max, midpoint, width (max − min) and width/midpoint;
+   - histograms per company and per level;
+   - how many distinct (min, max) pairs there are and how often the top pairs repeat.
+
+   Do the ranges "span too evenly"? For example, are widths a near-constant fraction of the midpoint? Are many postings sharing one identical range? Are ranges made up by our code (estimates, level bands, defaults, ±x% around a point, a currency conversion, rounding, ×12/×2080 basis conversion) rather than posted?
+2. **Provenance of every salary:**
+   - Is it parsed from the posting text, taken from a structured field, an estimate, or a level-band fill? Count each source per board.
+   - Check every code path that can set or widen `salary.min/max` or `ranges[]`.
+3. **Pick salaries against the live postings.** Draw at least 15 random salaries per board (seeded; record the seed) plus the 10 most common ranges per board. Compare each with the posting's own pay text in the stored description, and, for at most 3 per board, with the live posting page. Fetch politely: honest User-Agent, ≥1 s between requests to a host, respect robots.txt, at most ~25 fetches in total. Quote the exact posted sentence.
+4. **Multi-location and multi-level postings.**
+   - Is a posting that lists several US pay zones (Google, Apple, Meta and Amazon do this) collapsed into one min-of-mins to max-of-maxes span? That would make ranges wide and similar.
+   - Which range is shown on the card, which in the chart, and which Juice uses?
+   - Compare with the published per-zone ranges.
+5. **Compare with public references** you can cite (company pay-transparency pages, or levels.fyi style ranges where the license allows; quote and link them). Are the shown ranges plausible per level? Never copy a dataset with a restrictive license; cite only.
+6. **Compare beta's served view with the raw snapshot:**
+   - Build with `node scripts/build-static.js --out <scratch>/dist` with the data linked.
+   - Diff `dist/beta/api/jobs/<slug>.json` (and any packed or worker format) against the snapshot per posting.
+   - Find any place where served min/max differ from stored min/max.
+
+**Deliverables (you own these; do not edit product code outside them):**
+- `docs/process/salary-audit-data.md`, your log in the TEMPLATE.md structure: brief, inputs, decisions, replayable steps, verification, gaps, change log. Save this prompt verbatim to `docs/process/prompts/salary-audit-data.md`.
+- `data/vetting/2026-10-11-salary-audit/` with:
+  - your scripts (`*.mjs`);
+  - per-board stats as JSON;
